---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageTitle from "../components/PageTitle.astro";
import { data, withBase } from "../utils/data";

const { meta } = data;
const a = meta.analysis;
const coded = { source: 0, filled: 0, new: 0 };
data.ghosts.forEach((g) => coded[g.coding]++);
const downloads = [
  { file: "ghosts.nex", label: "Character matrix (Nexus)" },
  { file: "ghosts.rooted.tre", label: "Rooted ML tree with UFBoot support (Newick)" },
  { file: "ghosts.iqtree", label: "IQ-TREE report" },
  { file: "ghosts.csv", label: "Cleaned taxon table (CSV)" },
  { file: "characters.csv", label: "Character definitions (CSV)" },
];
---

<Layout title="Methods · Ghost phylogeny, literally">
  <PageTitle title="Methods" kicker="From spreadsheet to tree">
    Every number on this site is computed by the pipeline in <code>ghostphylo/</code>, rerun by GitHub Actions whenever the data change.
  </PageTitle>

  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-3xl gap-6 leading-relaxed">
    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Data</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The source is <code>{meta.source}</code>: {meta.taxa} taxa, {meta.outgroups} of them outgroups from Malaysia, the Philippines, Thailand, Cambodia, and South Asia. Of the taxa, {coded.source} keep the codings of the original matrix, {coded.filled} had missing traits filled by folklore synthesis, and {coded.new} are new entries coded entirely by synthesis. These are marked ◆ throughout.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Characters</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Eighteen traits come straight from the workbook (0 absent, 1 present, 2 variable; for sex, 1 female and 2 male or unspecified). The body plan and the diet are each added as one unordered multistate character, so a body plan counts once rather than as many one-hot columns. Clade is left out because it is the grouping the tree tests. The {meta.taxa} taxa reduce to {meta.uniqueRows} distinct rows; taxa that share a row cannot be separated by any tree.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-kafan-300 dark:border-malam-700" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Character table (scrolls sideways on small screens)">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">Characters and their states</caption>
          <thead class="bg-kafan-200/70 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider dark:bg-malam-900">
            <tr><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2">#</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2">Character</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2">States</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              data.characters.map((c, i) => (
                <tr class="border-t border-kafan-200 align-top dark:border-malam-800">
                  <td class="px-3 py-1.5 tabular-nums">{i + 1}</td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-1.5 font-semibold">{c.label}</td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-1.5 muted">
                    {c.states.map((s, j) => `${data.symbols[j]} ${s}`).join("; ")}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Inference</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        {a.software} with ModelFinder (<code>-m MFP</code>) on the morphological data type selected <strong>{a.model}</strong> by BIC
        {a.bic !== null && <> ({a.bic.toFixed(1)})</>}. Support comes from {a.bootstrap}; the seed is {a.seed}, so reruns are reproducible. The log-likelihood of the best tree is {a.logLikelihood?.toFixed(2)}.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The tree is rooted on the largest clade made only of outgroups{meta.outgroupMonophyletic ? ", and here the outgroups do form one clade" : ". They do not form one clade: several outgroups nest among Indonesian relatives, as the Malay and Philippine flying heads did in the earlier 61-taxon tree"}. Of {meta.nodes} internal nodes, {meta.strongNodes} have ultrafast bootstrap of 95 or more and {meta.weakNodes} fall below 50, so treat the deep branching as unresolved.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Plates</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Each ghost's plate is drawn by <code>ghostphylo/svgs.py</code> from its record: the body plan sets the figure, the clade sets the ring colour, and coded traits add scenery, such as a banyan for forest spirits, <i>nisan</i> gravestones and frangipani for burial, a lit <i>joglo</i> roof for village haunts, <i>kepeng</i> coins for theft, and an incense burner for black magic. A hand-drawn file in <code>assets/ghosts/</code> replaces the generated plate for that ghost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Pipeline</h2>
      <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl bg-malam-950 p-4 text-sm text-kafan-100"><code>uv run ghostphylo all     # parse, encode, IQ-TREE, export, plates
cd web && bun run build   # this site</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The GitHub workflow installs IQ-TREE, runs the same commands, uploads the analysis files as a build artifact, and deploys the site to GitHub Pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Downloads</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5">
        {
          downloads.map((d) => (
            <li>
              <a href={withBase(`/data/${d.file}`)} class="font-semibold text-darah-700 underline underline-offset-2 dark:text-prada-300" download>{d.file}</a>
              <span class="muted"> · {d.label}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="glass p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Caveats</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Folklore is not inherited like genes; shared traits may come from shared film portrayals, trade routes, or religion as much as from common descent. Read the tree as a map of resemblance among tellings, not a history of the spirits themselves. Verify any entry against primary ethnographic sources before citing it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
